  subject: C-Loud Mouth002-CR
  body: "<b>C.N.A. \u2014 ANOMALY FIELD RECORD</b>\nCONTROLLED DOCUMENT \u2014 INTERNAL
    USE ONLY\n\n<b>DEPARTMENT:</b> Observation and Telemetry Unit (O.T.U.)\n<b>DOCUMENT
    TYPE:</b> Anomaly Identification & Response Record\n<b>DESIGNATION:</b> C-Loud
    Mouth002-CR\n<b>REGISTERED NAME:</b> LOUD MOUTH\n<b>THREAT LEVEL:</b> 4 \u2014
    CRITICAL\n<b>BEHAVIOR CLASS:</b> AGGRESSIVE\n<b>RESPONSE AUTHORITY:</b> C.R.U.
    \u2014 Containment and Response Unit\n<b>O.T.U. INTERVENTION:</b> NOT AUTHORIZED\n<b>RECORD
    STATUS:</b> ACTIVE\n\n<b>01. GENERAL DESCRIPTION</b>\nLoud Mouth is a humanoid
    anomaly distinguished by an abnormally enlarged oral cavity and a prominent mass
    located at the front of its neck.\nThe mass is believed to contain an oversized
    vocal apparatus capable of producing sound at exceptionally high intensity.\nAlthough
    its general body structure resembles that of a human, the proportions of its
    mouth and throat are inconsistent with normal human anatomy.\nPersonnel are advised
    not to identify this anomaly by body shape alone.\nAuditory activity remains
    the most reliable indication of its presence.\n\n<b>02. PHYSICAL CHARACTERISTICS</b>\nLoud
    Mouth possesses several identifiable physical features:\n\u2022 A humanoid body
    with proportions generally resembling those of an adult human.\n\u2022 An abnormally
    wide mouth capable of opening beyond normal anatomical limits.\n\u2022 A large,
    protruding structure in the throat region, believed to function as an enlarged
    vocal organ.\n\u2022 Visible movement or expansion of the throat structure during
    vocal activity.\nThe full anatomical function of the throat structure has not
    been established.\nDirect examination is restricted to authorized C.R.U. personnel.\n\n<b>03.
    OBSERVED BEHAVIOR</b>\nLoud Mouth exhibits aggressive behavior toward personnel
    and nearby equipment.\nThe anomaly is capable of producing high-intensity vocal
    emissions that may be detected across multiple monitored areas.\nThese emissions
    may occur intermittently.\nThe absence of sound must not be interpreted as confirmation
    that the anomaly has departed.\nLoud Mouth may remain stationary between vocalizations
    or relocate to another area.\nIts precise location must be established before
    a response team can be dispatched.\n\n<b>04. AUDITORY HAZARD</b>\nVocal emissions
    produced by Loud Mouth present an extreme hazard to personnel and facility infrastructure.\nDocumented
    or anticipated effects include:\n\u2022 Severe auditory trauma, including rupture
    of the tympanic membrane.\n\u2022 Potentially fatal injuries resulting from exposure
    to high-intensity sound.\n\u2022 Damage to surveillance equipment and electronic
    components.\n\u2022 Interruption or complete loss of camera and communication
    signals.\n\u2022 Fracture or shattering of glass and other vulnerable materials.\n\u2022
    Structural damage to objects exposed to sufficiently intense vocal emissions.\nThe
    effective range and maximum output of the anomaly remain undetermined.\nAudio
    distortion, sudden signal interference, and unexplained equipment failures may
    indicate nearby vocal activity.\nPersonnel must not attempt to measure the anomaly\u2019s
    sound output through direct exposure.\n\n<b>05. DETECTION PROCEDURE</b>\nLoud
    Mouth is most reliably located through its vocal emissions.\nO.T.U. personnel
    are required to monitor all assigned camera feeds for unusual auditory activity.\nUpon
    detecting a suspected emission:\n1. Identify the camera channel from which the
    sound originated.\n2. Inspect the corresponding area for visual confirmation.\n3.
    Compare the observed characteristics with the Field Manual.\n4. Establish the
    anomaly\u2019s current location.\n5. Transmit an immediate report to the C.R.U.\nDo
    not remain on a single camera feed longer than necessary.\nIf visual confirmation
    is unavailable, report the suspected location and indicate that identification
    remains unconfirmed.\n\n<b>06. RESPONSE PROCEDURE</b>\nLoud Mouth is classified
    as a Level 4 threat.\nO.T.U. personnel are not authorized to approach, suppress,
    or neutralize this anomaly.\nUpon positive identification, immediately contact
    the Containment and Response Unit.\nThe report must include:\n\u2022 Anomaly
    designation.\n\u2022 Camera channel.\n\u2022 Last confirmed location.\n\u2022
    Current activity, if observable.\nDo not delay the report to obtain additional
    footage.\nDo not attempt direct intervention.\nRemain at the assigned observation
    station and continue monitoring the affected area until further instructions
    are received.\nContainment and neutralization are the responsibility of the C.R.U.\n\n<b>07.
    FAILURE CONDITIONS</b>\nFailure to identify and report Loud Mouth before a critical
    vocal emission may result in fatal exposure and severe damage to observation
    equipment.\nLoss of a camera feed does not indicate successful containment.\nIf
    multiple camera feeds fail following an auditory event, personnel must report
    the last known location immediately.\nContinued observation is required whenever
    equipment remains operational.\n\n<b>08. O.T.U. PERSONNEL DIRECTIVE</b>\nLoud
    Mouth must be located and reported without delay.\nDo not pursue it.\nDo not
    attempt to silence it.\nDo not wait for a second vocal emission to confirm its
    presence.\nIts voice is sufficient evidence to initiate a response.\n\n<b>C.N.A.
    \u2014 OBSERVATION AND TELEMETRY UNIT</b>\nOBSERVE. IDENTIFY. REPORT.\n<i>Failure
    to follow established response procedures may result in personnel loss.</i>"
